  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Kana Ram vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:28159) on 10 July, 2024.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Kana Ram vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:28159) on 10 July, 2024 [2024:Rj-Jd:28159] High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan At Jodhpur S.B. Criminal Revision Petition No. 522/2006 Kana Ram ----Petitioner Versus State ----Respondent For Petitioner(s) : Mr. Vijay Purohit. For Respondent(s) : Mr. Vikram Sharma, Pp. Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Arun Monga Order(Oral) 10/07/2024 1.",
    "facts": "The matter pertains to an incident which occurred in the year 1999 for alleged offences under Sections 18(C)/27(B)(11), 22(1) (CCA)/22(3), 18-A/28, 27(D), 27(B)(1) & 18(A)(1) & 17(B)(D) of Drugs & Cosmetics Act and the present criminal revision petition has been pending since 2006. 2. Petitioner is assailing the legality and validity of the judgment/order dated 05.06.2006 passed by learned Sessions Judge, Jalore in Criminal Appeal No.15/2004, whereby the judgment/order dated 15.07.2004 passed by the learned Chief Judicial Magistrate, Jalore in Criminal Regular Case No.396/1999, convicting the revisionist-petitioners was upheld. The petitioner was convicted and sentenced as under:- Offence u/s. Sentence Fine Default 18(C)/27(B)(11) of Drugs 01-year S.I. Rs.500/- 15 days S.I. & Cosmetics Act 22(1)(CCA)/22(3) of 3 months' S.I. - - Drugs & Cosmetics Act 18-A/28 of Drugs& 3 months' S.I. - - Cosmetics Act 27(D) of Drugs& 1 year S.I. Rs.500/- 15 days' S.I. Cosmetics Act 27(B)(1) of Drugs & 1 Year S.I. Rs.500/- 15 days' S.I. [2024:RJ-JD:28159] (2 of 3) [CRLR-522/2006] Cosmetics Act Kana Ram vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:28159) on 10 July, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/125853293/ 1 18(A)(1) & 17(B)(D) of 1 Year S.I. Rs.500/- 15 days' S.I. Drugs & Cosmetics Act 3.",
    "arguments": "4. At the very outset, instead of addressing the arguments on the merits of the conviction and the sentence awarded to the petitioner, learned counsel for the petitioner seeks concession of probation under Section 18 of the Probation of Offenders Act. 5. In support of his arguments, he relies on a judgment rendered by this Court in the case of State of Rajasthan Vs. Subhash Chandra &Ors. (S.B. Criminal Revision No.648/2017) decided on 31.07.2017, the relevant part of which reads as follows: \"In the case at hand, the respondents were held guilty for the offence under Section 27D of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 which carries a minimum sentence of one year with a proviso that in case the Court so feels, for adequate and special reasons to be recorded in the judgment, it may impose sentence for a term lesser than one year. Thus, apparently minimum sentence of one year provided for the offence under Section 27D of the Act is not absolute and discretion has been left with the Court to impose a lesser sentence albeit after recording adequate and special reasons. Section 18 of the Probation of Offenders Act excludes applicability of the Act to section 31 of the Reformatory Schools Act, 1897, or sub- section (2) of section 5 of the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1947 or of any law in force in any State relating to juvenile offenders or Borstal Schools.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. At the very outset, instead of addressing the arguments on the merits of the conviction and the sentence awarded to the petitioner, learned counsel for the petitioner seeks concession of probation under Section 18 of the Probation of Offenders Act. 5. In support of his arguments, he relies on a judgment rendered by this Court in the case of State of Rajasthan Vs. Subhash Chandra &Ors. (S.B. Criminal Revision No.648/2017) decided on 31.07.2017, the relevant part of which reads as follows: \"In the case at hand, the respondents were held guilty for the offence under Section 27D of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 which carries a minimum sentence of one year with a proviso that in case the Court so feels, for adequate and special reasons to be recorded in the judgment, it may impose sentence for a term lesser than one year. Thus, apparently minimum sentence of one year provided for the offence under Section 27D of the Act is not absolute and discretion has been left with the Court to impose a lesser sentence albeit after recording adequate and special reasons.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Section 18 of the Probation of Offenders Act excludes applicability of the Act to section 31 of the Reformatory Schools Act, 1897, or sub- section (2) of section 5 of the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1947 or of any law in force in any State relating to juvenile offenders or Borstal Schools."
  },
